{
  "summary": "The previous proposal's two-change approach is pragmatic and mostly sound, but has a subtle flaw in pollUntilChanged and misses a simpler fix for initial load. (1) For initial load: the 'aggressive polling for first 10 seconds then back off to 5s' idea adds unnecessary complexity. The simpler fix is just to call smartPollFeatures() immediately after its definition (right after the setInterval line). The 5-second setInterval is fine as the steady-state cadence — the only real problem is the initial 0-to-5-second gap before the first interval fires. One immediate call closes that gap entirely. No dynamic interval switching needed. (2) For pollUntilChanged: comparing _lastFeaturesHTML to detect change is brittle because it conflates 'the pipeline processed MY action' with 'any state changed at all'. If another action or the pipeline's periodic broadcast happens to update features during the retry window, pollUntilChanged will see a change and stop — even if the user's specific mutation hasn't been applied yet. In practice this is unlikely to matter for single-user scenarios, but it means the 'deterministic enough' claim doesn't fully hold. That said, this is an acceptable trade-off — the alternative (tracking per-action expected outcomes) would be far too complex. The real risk is the opposite case: if the pipeline processes the action but the HTML output happens to be identical (e.g., editing a field to the same value), pollUntilChanged will exhaust all retries for no reason. Adding a simple 'force refresh after action' flag would handle this edge case more cleanly than polling for change. (3) The proposal correctly identifies that zero Go server changes is a virtue. The server-side state is already authoritative and the polling model works — the only issue is timing. Keeping the fix purely client-side means no risk of introducing server-side bugs or coupling."
}
